{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לעשות הישר בעיני ה'", :translit "la'asot hayashar b'einei Hashem", :gloss "to do what is right in the eyes of YHWH"} {:he "בנים אתם", :translit "banim atem", :gloss "you are children"} {:he "בזמן שעושין רצונו של מקום קרויין בנים", :translit "biz'man she'osin retzono shel makom kru'in banim", :gloss "when they do the will of the Omnipresent they are called children"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: proximity of 'you are children' to 'to do what is right in the eyes of YHWH'"}], :frame {:text "The principle that Israel are called children specifically when they act righteously is established in Tractate Kiddushin 36a, where the verse 'You are children of YHWH your God' (Deuteronomy 14:1) is cited and the position is taken that they are called children when their deeds are proper.", :sources ["Kiddushin 36a" "Deuteronomy 14:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ונתן לך רחמים ורחמך", :translit "venatan lach rachamim verachamcha", :gloss "and grant you compassion and have compassion upon you"} {:he "בנים אתם", :translit "banim atem", :gloss "you are children"} {:he "מת", :translit "met", :gloss "dead"} {:he "עכו\"ם חשובה כמת", :translit "akum cheshuvah kamet", :gloss "idolatry is considered like a dead person"} {:he "היו מתגודדים לפני עכו\"ם כמו על מת", :translit "hayu mitgodedim lifnei akum k'mo al met", :gloss "they would gash themselves before idolatry like over a dead"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: proximity of 'you are children' to 'and grant you compassion'"} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: proximity of the word מת to the passage of the wayward city"}], :frame {:text "The link between human compassion and divine compassion appears in Shabbat 151a: 'Whoever is merciful to creatures, Heaven is merciful to him.' The comparison of idolaters to the dead derives from Ezekiel 36:25–26, interpreted in Avodah Zarah 5a to mean that one who worships idols is considered dead. 